\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S5}{\ignorespaces Human sign-to-text references remove most of the replay advantage on the matched high-confidence subset ($n=461$; released evaluator, canonical hypotheses). (a) Corpus BLEU-4 under original vs.\ human references: REC 14.94 $\to $ 7.35, PURE 24.98 $\to $ 8.30; the reference-frame intervention contrast reduces the gap $+10.03 \to +0.95$ (90.5\%). (b) Bootstrap distribution of the intervention contrast (10{,}000 paired resamples; mean 9.08, 95\% CI $[7.44, 10.86]$). (c) PURE$-$REC gap under original vs.\ human references across BLEU-4, BLEU-1, and chrF. References are back-translations by a single deaf fluent L2 DGS signer\nobreakspace  {}\cite  {czehmann2026sacred}; replacement cannot distinguish reference-text defects, paraphrase shift, BLEU floor effects, or disruption of the lexical coupling.\relax }}{18}{figure.caption.95}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:s5_ref_frame}{{S5}{18}{Human sign-to-text references remove most of the replay advantage on the matched high-confidence subset ($n=461$; released evaluator, canonical hypotheses). (a) Corpus BLEU-4 under original vs.\ human references: REC 14.94 $\to $ 7.35, PURE 24.98 $\to $ 8.30; the reference-frame intervention contrast reduces the gap $+10.03 \to +0.95$ (90.5\%). (b) Bootstrap distribution of the intervention contrast (10{,}000 paired resamples; mean 9.08, 95\% CI $[7.44, 10.86]$). (c) PURE$-$REC gap under original vs.\ human references across BLEU-4, BLEU-1, and chrF. References are back-translations by a single deaf fluent L2 DGS signer~\cite {czehmann2026sacred}; replacement cannot distinguish reference-text defects, paraphrase shift, BLEU floor effects, or disruption of the lexical coupling.\relax }{figure.caption.95}{}}
